# Gateway Rules

Local OpenAI-compatible HTTP server on **`127.0.0.1` only** (default port 4001).
Public reachability is via tunnel / BYO URL — see `tunnel.mdc`.

## Key model (upstream ×2 + gateway)

| Key                       | Config field           | Who sees it                          |
| ------------------------- | ---------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| Moonshot / Kimi API key   | `moonshotKeyEncrypted` | App only → Authorization to Moonshot |
| DeepSeek API key          | `deepseekKeyEncrypted` | App only → Authorization to DeepSeek |
| Thaura AI API key         | `thauraKeyEncrypted`   | App only → Authorization to Thaura   |
| Z.AI API key              | `zaiKeyEncrypted`      | App only → Authorization to Z.AI      |
| Gateway key (`sk-{alnum}`) | `gatewayKey`           | Cursor as “OpenAI API Key”           |

Never log or return upstream provider keys. Wrong gateway key → OpenAI-shaped 401.
Routing picks which upstream key to use from the **model map** (alias → provider).

### Upstream base URLs (code SoT)

Hardcoded defaults + optional env overrides live in
`internal/config/urls.go` (`UpstreamBaseURL`, `ChatCompletionsURL`).

| Provider | Default base                 |
| -------- | ---------------------------- |
| moonshot | `https://api.moonshot.ai/v1` |
| deepseek | `https://api.deepseek.com`   |
| thaura   | `https://backend.thaura.ai/v1` |
| zai       | `https://api.z.ai/api/coding/paas/v4` |

Use those helpers so Moonshot’s `/v1` and DeepSeek’s host root never produce
`/v1/v1/...`. Do not scatter raw URL string literals in the gateway proxy.

## Endpoints (MVP)

| Endpoint                    | Purpose                                              |
| --------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
| `GET /health`               | Status, metrics, URLs, which provider keys present   |
| `GET\|POST /v1/models`      | Advertise aliases + real ids (+ provider)            |
| `POST /v1/chat/completions` | Main path                                            |
| `POST /v1/responses`        | Same handler after Responses→Chat adapt              |
| Other `/v1/*`               | Proxy when cheap (embeddings/files) — provider-aware |

## Model map (alias → provider → real id)

Switching providers/models for the user is **choosing the Cursor alias** (or a
listed real id). One table drives sanitize policy, upstream base URL, API key,
and pricing row.

### Primary Cursor aliases (Agent-first)

| Cursor alias     | Provider | Real model          | Typical use         |
| ---------------- | -------- | ------------------- | ------------------- |
| `gpt-4o`         | moonshot | `kimi-k3`           | Planning / flagship |
| `gpt-4o-mini`    | moonshot | `kimi-k2.7-code`  | Coding; always thinks           |
| `o1`             | deepseek | `deepseek-v4-pro`   | Harder execution    |
| `o3-mini`        | deepseek | `deepseek-v4-flash` | Cheap execution     |
| `gpt-5-nano`     | thaura   | `thaura`            | Ethical AI; optional provider |
| `gpt-4.1-turbo`  | zai      | `glm-5.2`           | Planning; cheaper than K3    |
| `gpt-4.1`        | zai      | `glm-4.7`           | Cheap execution              |
| `gpt-4-turbo`    | zai      | `glm-5.2`           | **Compat alias** — Cursor sometimes rewrites `gpt-4.1-turbo` → `gpt-4-turbo` |

Set the alias manually in Cursor Settings → Models. The gateway maps it and
picks the right base URL + API key. No auto-application to Cursor's state.vscdb
— operators configure Cursor by hand (see `cursor-settings.mdc`).

### `Cursor rewrites a model ID (model picker ≠ sent model)`

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
| ------- | ----- | ---- |
| Model picker shows `gpt-4.1-turbo`, but the gateway logs `unknown model alias "gpt-4-turbo"` | Cursor performs its own client-side model-ID matching against its internal OpenAI catalog and may substitute a close/known ID (drops the `.1`) before sending | (1) Advertise the real IDs in `/v1/models`; (2) accept the rewritten ID as a **compat alias** mapping to the same real model (`gpt-4-turbo` → `glm-5.2`). Do not treat the rewritten name as a gateway bug. |

## Sanitizer order (preserve)

1. Map Cursor alias → **{provider, real model}** (table above)
2. Apply **provider- and model-aware** thinking / sampling policy
   (`kimi.mdc` or `deepseek.mdc`)
3. Map `max_completion_tokens` → `max_tokens`; clamp per model context
4. Strip incompatible OpenAI/Cursor sampling fields
5. Adapt Responses `input`/`instructions` → `messages`
6. Repair tool_call ids / pairing; sanitize tool names + JSON Schema
7. Seed empty probe message when tools present and messages empty
8. Message role fixes (`developer`→`system`); reasoning placeholder for tool-call assistants when required

Then proxy to the resolved provider base URL with that provider’s key. Stream
what Cursor asked for (passthrough SSE or synthesize). Retry once on upstream
400s mentioning `tool_call_id` (when that provider returns them).

Behavioral checklist (Moonshot-oriented reference):
`examples/use-kimi-on-cursor/src-tauri/tests/`. DeepSeek: OpenAI shapes in
`examples/openai-openapi/` + `deepseek.mdc`.

### `Cursor Agent tool schema rejected`

| Symptom                                   | Cause                                                    | Fix                                              |
| ----------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
| Upstream 400 on tools / empty Agent turns | MCP names, deep schemas, Responses shape, empty messages | Run full sanitizer order; seed probe; repair ids |

### `Wrong upstream after alias switch`

| Symptom                                                       | Cause                                                | Fix                                                            |
| ------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Request hits Moonshot while alias is DeepSeek (or vice versa) | Alias map missing provider field; hardcoded base URL | Resolve provider from map every request; select base URL + key |

### Go sanitizer library (`internal/gateway`)

Code SoT for alias resolve + request sanitize (T04+): `ResolveModel`,
`SanitizeRequest` → `SanitizeResult` with `Provider`, `Model`, and sanitized
`Body`. Unknown Cursor alias → error (HTTP layer maps to 400). **Strip order:**
apply provider thinking policy **before** stripping `reasoning_effort` — K3 must
keep `reasoning_effort: "max"`; do not copy reference K2 “strip effort then
inject thinking” blindly. K3: no `reasoning_content` placeholder injection.

### `Image content in a request (all providers)`

Cursor Agent resends screenshots / image content (`image_url`, `input_image`,
`image_file`) in message history. The gateway no longer strips images — it
describes every image via the vision worker.

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
| ------- | ----- | ---- |
| Image cannot be described (vision model key missing, 5-hour rate-limit hit, or upstream rejection) | The vision worker (`glm-4.6v`) is the only path for images, and it is temporarily or permanently unavailable | **Graceful fallback.** The image is replaced with an honest placeholder note (`[An image could not be described …]`) and the request proceeds to the text model — a rate-limited vision provider never blocks the conversation. Historical images still resolve from the durable cache with no vision call. |
| Image request succeeds but the text model responds with an image description | Image content was translated to text by the vision worker (Z.AI `glm-4.6v`) before proxying | Expected. Vision applies to **all providers** (moonshot/deepseek/zai/thaura), even ones with native vision support. Text models never receive raw `image_url` parts. |
| Remote `https://` image URLs | Fetch happens upstream-side by the vision model | Hashed by URL string for caching; data URIs are hashed by decoded bytes |
| Repeated identical images | Content-hash cache (SHA-256) — in-memory 10 min TTL + a **durable** SQLite cache (`{dataRoot}/usage/vision_cache.db`); `singleflight`-deduped | Second identical image within TTL is served from cache with no upstream call; a historical image already described on any prior turn/process is reused **without** a vision call |
| A previously-sent image seems to be re-described every turn | Cursor resends raw `image_url` parts in message history, so the gateway must resolve each image again; the content hash should match a cached entry and skip the vision call | Confirm the image hash is stable (logged via `vision_image_unavailable` / `vision_call_failed` `image_hash`). If the hash changes each turn (fresh data URI / signed URL), content-hash caching cannot dedupe it — investigate why Cursor varies the payload. Identical bytes (same hash) are served from the durable cache with no vision call. |

`vision.Describer.ReplaceImages` is the single pre-proxy pass, invoked from
`handleChatCompletions` (call site `internal/gateway/proxy.go`), after
`SanitizeRequest` and before `OptimizeRequest`.
It is installed for every provider; there is **no** provider gate. Descriptions
are persisted to a durable cache (`{dataRoot}/usage/vision_cache.db`), so an
image already described on a prior turn is reused without re-calling the vision
model. `ReplaceImages` is **graceful**: it never returns a fatal error for an
image it cannot describe. Historical images resolve from cache with no vision
call, and a new image that cannot be described (missing key, rate limit,
network, upstream rejection) is replaced with an honest placeholder note so the
request proceeds — a rate-limited vision model no longer blocks any turn,
whether or not it carries images.

> **Do not** reintroduce the old fail-fast behavior for images (surface
> `vision_error` / HTTP 400 and abort the request). The honest-placeholder
> fallback is the contract; the previous `[image omitted]` silent-strip behavior
> is also deprecated. The image hash is logged (`vision_image_unavailable`,
> `vision_call_failed` `image_hash`) to diagnose whether Cursor is resending
> stable or varying image bytes.

### `Cursor masks the real user prompt in XML and structured tags`

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
| ------- | ----- | --- |
| Content-based request classification (e.g. `simple_lookup` vs `complex_reasoning`) never fires for main-agent traffic — all requests classify as `complex_reasoning`, `editing`, or `unknown`, and `stripped_len = 0` | Cursor sends each user message as an **array of content parts**: part 0 is a huge context block (`<open_and_recently_viewed_files>`, `<attached_files>`, `<code_selection>`, `<terminal_selection>`) and part 1 holds `<timestamp>` + the real prompt wrapped in `<user_query>...</user_query>`. Blanket XML stripping removes `<user_query>` including its content, leaving nothing to classify. A summary block (`[Previous conversation summary]…`) is also inserted when Cursor compacts long chats | Extract the prompt from `<user_query>` **before** stripping noise: 1. Concatenate all text parts. 2. If `<user_query>…</user_query>` exists, keep only its inner text (`regexp.MustCompile`(`(?s)<user_query>\s*(.*?)\s*</user_query>`)). 3. Then strip remaining XML blocks + the `[Previous conversation summary]` block. 4. If the result is empty, classify as `unknown` (conservative: keep the model). **Do not** walk backwards through older messages to find a non-empty one — that misclassifies on stale content |
| Cursor subagents appear indistinguishable from main agent at the HTTP level — same system prompt length (~15k chars), same `has_tools=true`, same message structure | Cursor does not expose any HTTP-level signal (header, metadata, timestamp gap, model alias) that reliably identifies subagent traffic. The system prompt is the same for both subagent and main agent in current Cursor versions | Do not attempt heuristic subagent detection (message count, prompt length). Route all traffic through content-based classification — a subagent asking "find all test files" will correctly hit `code_search` and downgrade. Subagents send independent chat-completion requests, so their operation request is classified on its own |

### `Classify-and-override: precedence decides whether a downgrade fires`

Content-based classifier (`SubAgentRouter`) must pick the intent, then downgrade only
when the model has a mapped cheaper variant:

- Check order: structured output (`json_object`) → automation → editing → complex
  reasoning → code search → simple lookup. Classify on the **stripped** prompt
  (see `Cursor masks…` above), never raw.
- Downgrade-safe classes: `simple_lookup`, `code_search`, `structured_extraction`,
  `automation`. Keep the model for `editing` and `complex_reasoning`.
- A word like "fix" is a **strong-editing** signal and blocks automation → if a
  refactor needs pro, "refactor and create a PR" stays on the original model.
  Mechanical work (lint/PR/git/scripts) still downgrades even when "fix" co-occurs
  (`isLintTask` bypasses the strong-editing guard).

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
| ------- | ----- | --- |
| "fix linter errors in some files" stays on the expensive model instead of flash | `isStrongEditing` matches "fix" and blocks the `automation` downgrade | Let lint tasks classify as automation even with a strong-editing word present (`isLintTask` → bypass the `!isStrongEditing` guard) |
| `glm-5.2` / `kimi-k3` never downgrade on automation tasks | Model has no mapped cheaper variant and falls back to `deepseek-v4-flash` (`defaultFlashModel`). Override also changes provider (re-resolved via `ResolveModel`), so cross-provider routing works: e.g. `glm-5.2` → `deepseek-v4-flash` routes to `provider=deepseek`, not Z.AI. To add a same-provider cheap variant (e.g. `glm-4.7` as flash for `glm-5.2`), **add** an entry to `modelOverrideMap` (it currently only holds `"deepseek-v4-pro": "deepseek-v4-flash"` — there is nothing to uncomment). Entries take priority over the universal fallback. |

#### Verbosity control — model-keyed request-side transforms

Verbosity control (`internal/gateway/verbosity/`, wired as `Server.verbosity`) is
per-model and toggleable live from the **Output Verbosity** dashboard card
(`GET/PUT /api/verbosity`). Each model's toggle is read per-request via
`LiveSettings.VerbosityFor(model)` (`s.verbosityEnabledFor(model)`). The
controller is always allocated and gated per-request on that per-model toggle.
It coerces the model to be less verbose via two **request-side** mechanisms, all
keyed on the **served model**. It **never edits response content** — both
streaming and non-streaming replies pass through byte-for-byte:

- **Directive injection** — appends `flashTersenessDirective` to the last
  system/developer message (or prepends a new one). Constraints include a
  reasoning-before-action rule ("state what you are about to do in one line,
  then act; do not narrate your investigative process").
- **Token cap** — lowers `max_tokens` to `flashVerbosityMaxTokens` (4096);
  never raises.

| Aspect | Detail |
| ------ | ------ |
| Runtime toggle | `/api/verbosity` → per-model, applies immediately, no restart |
| Controller | Always allocated; per-request gate `s.verbosityEnabledFor(model)` |
| Model key | Config map keys on the **real** model id (`deepseek-v4-flash`, `deepseek-v4-pro`), not alias |
| Response handling | Both streaming and non-streaming replies pass through unchanged — verbosity only prompts/caps, never trims |
| Fail-open | Unknown model / no config → passthrough unchanged |

### `Model-dependent middlewares must run AFTER routing/downgrade`

Request transforms that are keyed on the served model (verbosity, thinking
shape, token caps) must be applied **after** the subagent-router
`ClassifyAndOverride` downgrade reassigns the model — not at the point where the
sanitizer first resolved the alias.

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
| ------- | ----- | --- |
| Verbosity controls never fire on a turn the router downgraded to `deepseek-v4-flash` (directive absent, `max_tokens` uncapped) | `ApplyRequest` ran with the **original** resolved model (`deepseek-v4-pro` for `o1`); the controller config keys only on `deepseek-v4-flash`, so it early-returned | In `handleChatCompletions`, apply model-keyed request transforms **after** `ClassifyAndOverride` (and after the provider re-resolve) so they see the final served model |

